WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:02.280
Instructor: So now we get to talk about pull requests.

00:02.280 --> 00:04.980
This is the first of a few videos on the topic.

00:04.980 --> 00:06.270
I'm just gonna start in this video

00:06.270 --> 00:09.660
by talking about what pull requests or PRs are

00:09.660 --> 00:12.240
and why we want them or why they exist.

00:12.240 --> 00:13.650
And then in subsequent videos,

00:13.650 --> 00:14.970
we'll make our own.

00:14.970 --> 00:18.570
Okay, so we talked about this concept of working on branches

00:18.570 --> 00:20.730
and how that makes our life a lot easier.

00:20.730 --> 00:21.960
But then at some point

00:21.960 --> 00:24.090
somebody needs to merge those changes in

00:24.090 --> 00:26.760
from a feature branch into some main branch,

00:26.760 --> 00:28.560
master main, whatever, right?

00:28.560 --> 00:31.110
We don't just stay on separate feature branches forever

00:31.110 --> 00:33.060
otherwise everyone would be working in a silo

00:33.060 --> 00:35.010
and never merging and sharing their code,

00:35.010 --> 00:37.860
which defeats the whole collaboration thing.

00:37.860 --> 00:40.530
So rather than just allowing a free for all merge,

00:40.530 --> 00:43.260
especially if you're at a company where the stakes are high

00:43.260 --> 00:46.110
and you can't afford to screw up something on the main

00:46.110 --> 00:49.050
or the master branch, you have users, you've got,

00:49.050 --> 00:51.060
I mean, even if not that,

00:51.060 --> 00:52.620
if you've got a hundreds of developers

00:52.620 --> 00:55.080
and somebody includes some bad commit

00:55.080 --> 00:57.540
or something that breaks things on the main branch,

00:57.540 --> 01:00.510
at the very least you've got a hundred something developers

01:00.510 --> 01:03.510
who now have a bad version of that main branch

01:03.510 --> 01:06.090
and they have to get rid of a commit or reset.

01:06.090 --> 01:07.320
It's just annoying.

01:07.320 --> 01:08.370
You wanna make sure

01:08.370 --> 01:11.040
that whatever you are merging in

01:11.040 --> 01:12.690
or whoever is doing the merging

01:12.690 --> 01:15.150
has some discussion around it,

01:15.150 --> 01:16.410
has some approval,

01:16.410 --> 01:18.180
maybe changes are made

01:18.180 --> 01:20.520
at the request of somebody else, right?

01:20.520 --> 01:22.860
There's some code review process involved,

01:22.860 --> 01:26.340
and pull requests or PRs are an integral part

01:26.340 --> 01:27.480
of that process.

01:27.480 --> 01:30.720
So pull requests are not a native Git feature.

01:30.720 --> 01:32.520
They are built on top

01:32.520 --> 01:35.550
of platforms like GitHub and Bitbucket and some others.

01:35.550 --> 01:38.610
But really most people are referring to GitHub these days.

01:38.610 --> 01:41.310
But again, not exclusive to GitHub

01:41.310 --> 01:45.330
and 100% not part of Git itself.

01:45.330 --> 01:47.070
So pull requests allow developers

01:47.070 --> 01:50.250
to tell other team members, other collaborators

01:50.250 --> 01:51.900
that they have some new work

01:51.900 --> 01:53.310
that they want to be reviewed,

01:53.310 --> 01:55.950
new work that is on a branch

01:55.950 --> 01:59.280
that they want to be discussed, accepted,

01:59.280 --> 02:02.340
AKA merged in or rejected.

02:02.340 --> 02:04.320
And this facilitates discussion.

02:04.320 --> 02:05.580
They can get feedback,

02:05.580 --> 02:09.660
they can even update the work on the same pull request.

02:09.660 --> 02:11.017
So think of it as,

02:11.017 --> 02:14.370
"Hey everybody on my team, hundreds of people

02:14.370 --> 02:17.790
I have this new branch with this feature I just completed

02:17.790 --> 02:19.260
or I think it's complete.

02:19.260 --> 02:20.093
It's on GitHub.

02:20.093 --> 02:22.320
I'm making this request, this pull request.

02:22.320 --> 02:24.930
Can you take a look at it and somebody,

02:24.930 --> 02:26.880
will you merge it in if it's good,

02:26.880 --> 02:29.250
or will you tell me if it's not good?"

02:29.250 --> 02:31.500
That's pretty much what a pull request is.

02:31.500 --> 02:34.080
So we do some work locally on a feature branch

02:34.080 --> 02:36.210
like we did in the last couple of videos.

02:36.210 --> 02:38.370
Stevie added the nav bar branch, right?

02:38.370 --> 02:39.540
We finished the nav bar,

02:39.540 --> 02:41.070
we push it up to GitHub.

02:41.070 --> 02:42.750
Instead of just merging that directly

02:42.750 --> 02:45.750
into master or into main,

02:45.750 --> 02:46.770
and a lot of companies,

02:46.770 --> 02:48.450
actually the main branch is protected.

02:48.450 --> 02:49.680
I'll show you how to do that.

02:49.680 --> 02:51.360
You can't just merge stuff in.

02:51.360 --> 02:53.490
You actually have to make a pull request

02:53.490 --> 02:55.770
so that there's an opportunity to discuss

02:55.770 --> 02:58.860
and accept or reject your changes.

02:58.860 --> 03:00.360
Somebody makes that decision.

03:00.360 --> 03:03.000
Maybe multiple people actually have to consent

03:03.000 --> 03:05.100
and actually approve of your changes.

03:05.100 --> 03:06.570
So once you make the pull request,

03:06.570 --> 03:08.310
you then have to wait for it to be approved

03:08.310 --> 03:10.770
and hopefully merged or something.

03:10.770 --> 03:12.720
Discussion, it might be closed.

03:12.720 --> 03:15.480
It might be ignored, but hopefully not.

03:15.480 --> 03:17.580
So here's an example with some diagrams.

03:17.580 --> 03:18.690
I'm working on this feature.

03:18.690 --> 03:21.210
I finish it, I push it up to GitHub,

03:21.210 --> 03:23.370
and I wanna open a pull request.

03:23.370 --> 03:27.090
So on GitHub there's this new branch, my new feature,

03:27.090 --> 03:29.040
and you've probably seen this before,

03:29.040 --> 03:30.330
compare and pull request.

03:30.330 --> 03:31.620
It showed up a couple of times

03:31.620 --> 03:33.210
when you push branches up.

03:33.210 --> 03:35.580
That is a button I definitely want to click

03:35.580 --> 03:37.080
to make a pull request,

03:37.080 --> 03:39.300
or I can also click it here.

03:39.300 --> 03:41.310
There's a couple of different ways of doing it.

03:41.310 --> 03:43.830
But if I click pull request,

03:43.830 --> 03:45.720
I then have this option to choose

03:45.720 --> 03:47.730
what pull request am I making?

03:47.730 --> 03:50.250
Well, I am requesting that my branch,

03:50.250 --> 03:53.460
my new feature be merged in to master

03:53.460 --> 03:55.680
or to main or whatever the default

03:55.680 --> 03:57.270
or it could be any other base branch.

03:57.270 --> 04:00.600
But typically we're talking about master slash main.

04:00.600 --> 04:03.330
So then I need to provide some information, right?

04:03.330 --> 04:07.530
I need to come up with a name for what my pull request is.

04:07.530 --> 04:08.850
What's the reason for it?

04:08.850 --> 04:10.350
What does it include?

04:10.350 --> 04:12.300
Depending on the company and the organization

04:12.300 --> 04:13.980
there can be some pretty strict rules

04:13.980 --> 04:15.270
for what goes in here.

04:15.270 --> 04:17.310
And then I can click create pull request,

04:17.310 --> 04:18.510
and then I wait.

04:18.510 --> 04:20.790
So in this hypothetical world of these slides

04:20.790 --> 04:21.623
I have a boss

04:21.623 --> 04:25.440
and my boss is the only one who can approve changes,

04:25.440 --> 04:27.930
who can actually merge pull requests in on GitHub.

04:27.930 --> 04:30.450
There are different ways of configuring permissions

04:30.450 --> 04:32.370
and all of that that we can get into.

04:32.370 --> 04:35.970
But basically my boss has to accept my pull request.

04:35.970 --> 04:38.460
I as just an employee,

04:38.460 --> 04:41.640
I actually don't have the ability to merge directly

04:41.640 --> 04:42.990
into master on GitHub.

04:42.990 --> 04:45.030
I'm supposed to make a pull request.

04:45.030 --> 04:47.610
So my boss finds some issues

04:47.610 --> 04:49.620
and she actually leaves some feedback.

04:49.620 --> 04:51.780
She asked me to make some changes,

04:51.780 --> 04:53.040
pinging me when it's ready.

04:53.040 --> 04:57.120
I can notify people or I can mention people in comments.

04:57.120 --> 04:59.460
I can attach files.

04:59.460 --> 05:02.340
She can even just close this out by the way.

05:02.340 --> 05:04.290
Just close it without comment.

05:04.290 --> 05:07.110
Nope, it's a bit of a blunt way of saying no.

05:07.110 --> 05:08.820
Or she can just leave the comments.

05:08.820 --> 05:11.190
So then I can respond.

05:11.190 --> 05:13.140
We can have a conversation about it, right?

05:13.140 --> 05:15.840
I can say, "Okay, sounds good.

05:15.840 --> 05:17.670
I'll get to work on blah, blah, blah, blah, blah."

05:17.670 --> 05:19.297
Or I can say something like,

05:19.297 --> 05:21.390
"Hmm, are you sure you really want me to do that?

05:21.390 --> 05:23.250
What's the motivation?

05:23.250 --> 05:25.410
I mean, I'm not sure I agree with that.

05:25.410 --> 05:27.480
What do you think other developer?

05:27.480 --> 05:30.150
And then I can bring somebody else in and we can have

05:30.150 --> 05:33.810
a full discussion here as a team, okay?"

05:33.810 --> 05:37.170
I can even make more changes if she requests them.

05:37.170 --> 05:38.970
But then eventually, hopefully

05:38.970 --> 05:41.490
my boss decides things are good to go,

05:41.490 --> 05:43.680
it's time to merge those changes in.

05:43.680 --> 05:45.810
So if she accepts my pull request,

05:45.810 --> 05:48.930
she can then go and click merge pull request

05:48.930 --> 05:50.010
and merge my changes

05:50.010 --> 05:52.020
or whoever's in charge of doing the merging,

05:52.020 --> 05:54.450
merge my branch into the main branch,

05:54.450 --> 05:56.040
the branch I was requesting,

05:56.040 --> 05:57.900
and then we're good to go.

05:57.900 --> 05:59.280
So she'll click that button

05:59.280 --> 06:02.250
and then I'll demonstrate this for real,

06:02.250 --> 06:03.600
not just with slides.

06:03.600 --> 06:06.300
Then she can make the merge commit message directly

06:06.300 --> 06:07.530
on GitHub.

06:07.530 --> 06:11.850
So this is just a mechanism for merging changes in

06:11.850 --> 06:13.980
from a feature branch into some other branch

06:13.980 --> 06:15.870
where instead of just going for it,

06:15.870 --> 06:17.790
we have this process

06:17.790 --> 06:19.320
where we we can have conversations,

06:19.320 --> 06:20.310
we can chat with each other,

06:20.310 --> 06:22.500
we can make updates, request things.

06:22.500 --> 06:25.590
We can reject a suggestion or a pull request,

06:25.590 --> 06:26.910
or we can accept it.

06:26.910 --> 06:28.470
But if my pull request is accepted

06:28.470 --> 06:30.990
the end result is still just my branch is merged

06:30.990 --> 06:32.160
into another branch.

06:32.160 --> 06:34.830
It's just a slower, longer process

06:34.830 --> 06:38.670
that is more transparent and more controlled, I guess,

06:38.670 --> 06:40.080
for merging.

06:40.080 --> 06:42.840
So that's an intro to the concept of pull requests.

06:42.840 --> 06:44.310
I'll wrap up by showing you

06:44.310 --> 06:48.480
that if we look at any popular repository on GitHub

06:48.480 --> 06:49.710
there's going to be a section

06:49.710 --> 06:52.170
for pull requests like this is React,

06:52.170 --> 06:55.890
a very popular repository, lots of contributors.

06:55.890 --> 06:59.670
Those contributors don't just directly push changes up.

06:59.670 --> 07:01.170
They don't even have permission

07:01.170 --> 07:05.220
to directly push changes to any of the branches here.

07:05.220 --> 07:07.140
There's another way of making pull requests.

07:07.140 --> 07:08.970
We're gonna talk about involving something called

07:08.970 --> 07:10.740
the Fork and Clone Workflow.

07:10.740 --> 07:12.360
That will be coming up in a little bit.

07:12.360 --> 07:14.100
It's a common pattern to contribute

07:14.100 --> 07:15.150
to open source projects

07:15.150 --> 07:17.040
where you're not an actual collaborator,

07:17.040 --> 07:18.840
you don't have permission to push.

07:18.840 --> 07:20.040
Anyway, we'll get there.

07:20.040 --> 07:21.750
But if we go to pull requests

07:21.750 --> 07:23.730
we can see quite a few of them here.

07:23.730 --> 07:25.920
And again, there's a lot of structure

07:25.920 --> 07:28.230
and patterns that have to be followed

07:28.230 --> 07:29.820
when you're creating a pull request

07:29.820 --> 07:32.280
for a big open source project.

07:32.280 --> 07:36.000
So they all follow particular naming conventions.

07:36.000 --> 07:38.400
They all have these different tags.

07:38.400 --> 07:39.810
But you can see, here's one,

07:39.810 --> 07:42.150
somebody wants to merge one commit into master

07:42.150 --> 07:43.650
from their feature branch.

07:43.650 --> 07:45.270
Notice the name of this feature branch.

07:45.270 --> 07:47.310
Well, this is not a great one actually

07:47.310 --> 07:48.270
from Dependabot.

07:48.270 --> 07:49.440
If I find another one,

07:49.440 --> 07:51.453
how about this one here?

07:52.740 --> 07:55.920
So this is a pull request from the branch

07:55.920 --> 08:00.920
Rick Hanlon II, Hanlonii, I don't know.

08:00.960 --> 08:03.000
Rh-add-effect-warn-test.

08:03.000 --> 08:04.230
That's the feature branch.

08:04.230 --> 08:06.210
Whatever he did on this branch,

08:06.210 --> 08:08.460
he wants to merge it into master.

08:08.460 --> 08:11.190
And then we see some information

08:11.190 --> 08:13.980
about what his pull request includes.

08:13.980 --> 08:15.510
He wants to add a test

08:15.510 --> 08:18.000
so we can see under commits,

08:18.000 --> 08:20.640
here's the commit, we can view that commit,

08:20.640 --> 08:22.170
what's included, what's new.

08:22.170 --> 08:24.240
So some new code here

08:24.240 --> 08:26.040
and then there's a discussion around it.

08:26.040 --> 08:26.873
So this person said,

08:26.873 --> 08:28.830
"Please, this should probably throw an error,

08:28.830 --> 08:30.780
like how blah, blah, blah, blah, blah."

08:32.280 --> 08:34.260
It's a conversation in other words.

08:34.260 --> 08:35.790
And this one is still open,

08:35.790 --> 08:38.340
meaning it has not been closed or rejected,

08:38.340 --> 08:40.080
but it also has not been merged.

08:40.080 --> 08:44.280
Oh, and there's a lot more conversation here around this PR

08:44.280 --> 08:46.260
and that's kind of a good place to end,

08:46.260 --> 08:47.730
just to show you an example.

08:47.730 --> 08:50.580
So these larger projects have tons of pull requests.

08:50.580 --> 08:52.650
Sometimes it can take a little while to get yours

08:52.650 --> 08:54.240
if it's not super important,

08:54.240 --> 08:56.580
if it's not a critical feature you're working on

08:56.580 --> 08:58.050
it can take a little while to get noticed,

08:58.050 --> 09:00.780
depending on the resources available

09:00.780 --> 09:02.280
to the project maintainers.

09:02.280 --> 09:04.860
Anyway, that's the basic idea of a pull request.

09:04.860 --> 09:07.110
I'll show you the workflow in the next video.
